\pard \ql {\b EP1: Intro}\par
\pard \ql {\b \u187?}\par
\pard \ql {\b EP1S21}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b For the next 7 weeks, we will look at the stories of these women -- overlooked by man, but precious to God. Yeshua met them, saw them, and changed their story forever. And he can do the same for you.}\par
\pard \ql {\b Join us next time as we visit the story of the Samaritan woman at the well.}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b \u187?EP2: SAMARITAN WOMAN AT WELL}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b EP2S5}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b Now the picture of a Jewish man at a well is not foreign to us. It calls to mind a familiar biblical scene: meeting a bride. Abrahams servant found a bride for Isaac; Jacob met Rachel; Moses met Zipporah; all at the well.}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b \u187?EP2S8}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b The hostility between Jews and Samaritans ran deep.  A long history of enmity was meeting at that moment. And not only was she a Samaritan; this woman also had a reputation. Five former husbands, and the sixth man in her life was not her husband. Little did she know that this seventh Man would be unlike any other she had encountered.}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b \u187?EP2S13}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b Who is this Man and why is he talking to me? She asked him several questions: how can you draw water without anything to draw with? And who are you? Are you greater than Jacob, our father?}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b \u187?EP2S15}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b In an Ancient Jewish Wedding System, the Bridegroom offered the Chosen Bride a gift  something of value.  This is the bridal price known as the Mohar.  And here, Yeshua as the Ultimate Bridegroom offered her The Ultimate Gift. One of great value: The Holy Spirit as a dwelling fountain of life and satisfaction.}\par
\pard \ql {\b Give me that water she asked so I will not be thirsty}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b \u187?EP2S17}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b In John ch.1, Nathanael asked Yeshua, How do you know me? And Yeshua revealed to him that, not only did he know him. But he had seen him under the fig tree before they ever met.  In the same way, Yeshua assured this woman that he knew everything about her.  Yeshua always gives what people really need, because he truly knows us and knows everything about us.}\par
\pard \ql {\b \u187?}\par
\pard \ql {\b EP2S19}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b It was now clear to her that this was no ordinary man. She had encountered someone - greater. Surely he could even solve a long-standing theological conflict. Who is worshipping God rightly  we Samaritans, or the Jews. But Yeshua assures her, Salvation is from the Jews. This wasn't just a side comment. It was key to receiving this living water. It would be the Jewish Messiah that would cross the great cultural divide in order to bring salvation to the world.}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b \u187?EP2S20}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b And then the unexpected happens. She made a declaration of faith. "I know that Messiah is coming. When he comes, he will tell us all things." And at that moment Yeshua reveals himself to her with one of the clearest revelations in all the Gospels: "I who speak to you am he."}\par
\pard \ql {\b In the original language, Yeshua used the words I AM -- the same words God spoke to Moses from the burning bush. He was not just claiming to be a prophet. He was declaring himself as God.}\par
\pard \ql {\b \u187?}\par
\pard \ql {\b EP2S23}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b Yeshua chose a nameless Samaritan woman, with a painful past, to join him in ministry.  Her testimony brought the revival of an entire city. Many Samaritans believed based on her testimony, and many more when they encountered the savior of the world.}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b \u187?EP2S28}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b In order to pay the bride price, He performed a truly extraordinary act. Not of physical heroism, but of sacrificial love. He purchased His bride at the cost of His own life. He who offered living water, suffered thirst on the cross, so we would never thirst again.}\par
\pard \ql {\b Sometimes we think that God is focused on our past. Where weve come from. What weve done. But like this Samaritan woman, Yeshua sees deeper. He sees your deepest need  for living water.  And he paid the price for both on the cross.}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b \u187?EP2S33}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b John places the story at the well right after the story of Nicodemus. The contrast is striking. Nicodemus, a respected Pharisee, approached Yeshua secretly, at night. But Yeshua approached this woman in broad daylight.}\par
\pard \ql {\b Nicodemus went out his way to protect his reputation. Yeshua risked his reputation and went out of HIS way to reach this unlikely Samaritan woman. }\par
\pard \ql {\b He was an esteemed Jewish rabbi.}\par
\pard \ql {\b She was a foreigner, nameless, and outcast. But it was to her, that Yeshua declared: I AM he. I am the Messiah youve waited for. And she, the least likely witness, went and told an entire city.}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b \u187?EP2S36}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b Yeshua is not ashamed to be associated with you. He does not run away at the sight of your sin. He is not even ashamed to use us, flawed as we are, to reach others. You may feel like the least likely person that Yeshua would stop for. But His love meets us at our own well. He approaches the broken, seemingly unqualified, for His greatest work!}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b \u187?EP3: WIDOW AT NAIN}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b EP3S4}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b In ancient times, widows were especially vulnerable. They had no husband to advocate for them or look out for their interests. They were easy targets for exploitation. And now she had lost her only son  the only person left that would have cared for her. She was left defenseless.}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b \u187?EP3S5}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b Gods heart for widows is shown clearly in the Torah by establishing laws to protect them and care for them. And nowhere is this heart more wonderfully shown than the book of Ruth. In her story, we discover two widows, with no sons, and their struggle to survive. At the lowest point in their lives, Boaz the redeemer appears on the scene and history changed forever. Through Boaz and Ruth, the Redeemer of the World would come.}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b In the face of great suffering , the same heart for the vulnerable and broken walked towards this defenseless widow -- in the person of Yeshua.}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b \u187?}\par
\pard \ql {\b EP3S7}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b The words moved with compassion are rich in meaning. It's a gut-level, physical response of mercy and compassion. The Gospels record several times where Yeshua was moved with compassion.  But this is the only time where it says: he was moved with compassion for HER.  Her grief received his full attention.}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b \u187?EP3S8}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b Powerless and grieving, she suddenly heard the words of compassion spoken to her: do not cry.}\par
\pard \ql {\b The cries of the Son of God on the cross in his greatest suffering speak loudly today. As Isaiah wrote, Surely he has borne our griefs and carried our sorrows.  God himself entered our suffering: he feels our pain.}\par
\pard \ql {\b Yes, God is strongly moved by our suffering and grief.  In fact, in Romans 8 we learn that the Holy Spirit groans with us as we groan. Have you ever thought about the fact that God weeps with us? That He groans with us? Groans are a powerful expression of our greatest suffering and pain.}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b \u187?EP3S17}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b But Yeshua is far more than a prophet. Later, when Martha grieved for her brothers death, Yeshua declared to her: I am the resurrection and the life. Whoever believes in me, though he die, yet shall he live}\par
\pard \ql {\b Perhaps we will not experience the resurrection of our loved ones in our lifetime. But He does comfort us in our suffering here and now. And one day there will be an end to our groaning. The day when God 'will wipe away every tear from our eyes. When mourning, crying and pain will be no more' and 'death will be no more.}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b \u187?EP3S22}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b Today, we still live in a world where death and loss are real. God does perform miracles today. But the reality is that most of us will walk through grief and not receive our loved ones back -- not yet. The widow of Nain had a crowd surrounding her. Often, however, our pain outlasts the crowd. The grief remains.}\par
\pard \ql {\b But Yeshua remains too. He is faithful to walk into your life at the point of your greatest brokenness or hopelessness. When we encounter loss, we can also encounter Yeshua.}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b \u187?}\par
\pard \ql {\b EP3S24}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b In 2 Corinthians, Paul begins a blessing with these words: The Father of mercies and the God of all comfort. Sometimes we tend to skip over words of Scripture we have heard many times, and we miss their deep meaning for our lives.}\par
\pard \ql {\b Mercy and comfort are paired together here: as feeling and response. The father of Mercies responds with comfort. This means that our Father, the father of mercies, is moved actively toward us. It is not a passive feeling; it causes Him to act on our behalf.}\par
\pard \ql {\b Yeshua is this perfect representation of Gods mercy acting in a way that brings comfort. He draws near to us when were brokenhearted. In your time of heaviness, lift up your eyes. Yeshua is drawing near. EP3S25, EP3S26,}\par
\pard \ql {\b \u187?}\par
\pard \ql {\b EP3S30}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b Next time, we will meet another woman, broken by years of sin. And well discover the power of Gods forgiveness: bringing those farthest away, near to the very feet of Yeshua.}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b \u187?EP4: SINNER IN SIMON'S HOUSE}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b EP4S9}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b Repulsed by what was happening, Simon concluded that surely Yeshua could not be a true prophet of God. No true prophet would ever allow someone like her to touch him.}\par
\pard \ql {\b And then, to make matters worse, Yeshua declared:}\par
\pard \ql {\b Her sins, which are many, are forgiven. For she loved much. But he who is forgiven little, loves little."}\par
\pard \ql {\b This was a profound statement: Loving Yeshua is impossible unless we are forgiven -- and unless we realize how MUCH we are forgiven.}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b \u187?EP4S19}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b Yeshua often spoke in surprising ways. On one occasion he had words of rebuke to all those who considered themselves righteous:}\par
\pard \ql {\b "...the tax collectors and prostitutes enter the kingdom of God before you. For John came to you in the way of righteousness, and you did not believe him. But the tax collectors and prostitutes believed him. And even when you saw it, you did not afterward repent and believe him." What is this way of righteousness that the Pharisees refused to accept? It is the acknowledgment that I am a sinner. Its the act of repentance. This is the only way acceptable before God.}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b \u187?EP4S23}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b Selfrighteousness and spiritual pride keep us from repenting more than any other sin. Why? Because they convince us we have nothing to repent of. Yeshua made clear that humility before God is the way to come to Him. In contrast, self-sufficiency and pride prevent us from receiving Gods grace and from recognizing our deep need for a Savior. The only thing that keeps us from Yeshua is the belief that we dont need him.}\par
\pard \ql {\b \u187?}\par
\pard \ql {\b EP4S29}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b What is a true worshipper? In this story, the worshipper is the one who recognizes the immensity of their sin and the greater immensity of Gods mercy.}\par
\pard \ql {\b Yeshua illustrated this same truth in another parable  one that cuts even closer. He told of two men praying in the temple:}\par
\pard \ql {\b The Pharisee, standing by himself, prayed thus: God, I thank you that I am not like other men, extortioners, unjust, adulterers, or even like this tax collector\u8230? But the tax collector, standing far off, would not even lift up his eyes to heaven, but beat his breast, saying, God, be merciful to me, a sinner!}\par
\pard \ql {\b \u187?}\par
\pard \ql {\b EP4S31}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b Yeshua ended the parable with this completely unexpected verdict:}\par
\pard \ql {\b I tell you, this man went down to his house justified, rather than the other. For everyone who exalts himself will be humbled, but the one who humbles himself will be exalted.}\par
\pard \ql {\b True worship does not flow from propping up your own righteous acts. Worship flows from a humble heart that has encountered forgiveness.}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b \u187?}\par
\pard \ql {\b EP5: BLEEDING WOMAN}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b EP5S4}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b As Yeshua started towards Jairuss house, a massive crowd pressed on him from every side. Among this multitude of people, was a woman who had been bleeding for 12 years. Quietly and hidden from human eyes she reached out to touch Yeshuas garment. But she was not supposed to be there.}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b \u187?}\par
\pard \ql {\b EP5S8}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b But then she heard about Yeshua. The one that spoke to unclean spirits and commanded them to leave. Who touched unclean lepers, and even the dead, and healed them. She had one thought: if I could only touch his garment, I will be healed.}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b \u187?EP5S10}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b In the book of Numbers 15 we read that these tassels represented the law given to Israel at Sinai. The sons of Israel were required to wear these so that they would see it, and they would remember to obey the commandments of God. This woman was breaking one of those commandments in touching him with her uncleanness.}\par
\pard \ql {\b But in one touch of faith, what all the doctors couldnt do for 12 years - faith in Yeshua did. She was made whole. And at that moment, Yeshua felt power go out from him.}\par
\pard \ql {\b Under the law, the touch of the unclean defiled you. But when the unclean touched Yeshua the son of God, they became clean. The gospels record that many people with sicknesses and demon possession touched Yeshua and were healed. }\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b \u187?EP5S17}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b But Yeshuas timetable is different from ours. He has his fathers timetable in mind. And his father had his own daughter in the crowd. A daughter that still had a need. You see, the woman had been healed physically, but there was something deeper in her life that Yeshua wanted to give her. He cares for the whole person.}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b \u187?EP5S20}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b Daughter - a word that expresses closeness, belonging, intimacy, and love. This is the word that Yeshua wanted her to remember every day of her life. With this name, He was drawing her close to Himself, instead of pushing her away. He touches the deep need in her soul.}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b \u187?EP5S27}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b Notice that this miracle happened when Yeshua was on his way to heal Jairus daughter. While Yeshua was talking with the woman who touched his garment, the little girl died. But Yeshua has power and authority over death. He went into the home, and raised the girl to life.}\par
\pard \ql {\b Think of the contrast between these two events: Jairus a well respected and loved man, with high standing in the synagogue comes to Yeshua and meets him face on because he can.  The bleeding woman named by her sickness isolated and unwanted in the crowd - quietly approaches from behind.}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b \u187?}\par
\pard \ql {\b EP5S35}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b What is the one thing that paralyzes you? That makes you feel like you are in emotional and spiritual isolation. Maybe no one was able to help you. And Your situation has only worsened.}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b \u187?EP6: CANAANITE WOMAN}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b EP6S6}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b While Yeshua and his disciples were spending time alone in this Gentile region, there was an interruption to the quiet by the loud cries of a Canaanite woman.}\par
\pard \ql {\b Have mercy on me, O Lord, Son of David; my daughter is severely oppressed by a demon. , was her desperate plea.}\par
\pard \ql {\b She had heard what Yeshua could do. she had even heard that Yeshua had cast thousands of demons from a gentile like her. And if Yeshua could do it for him, then surely, he could do it for her daughter.}\par
\pard \ql {\b She called him Lord and addressed Him as Son of David.  This Canaanite woman recognized Yeshuas true identity. She used His Messianic title -- something many within Israel itself had refused to acknowledge.}\par
\pard \ql {\b \u187?}\par
\pard \ql {\b EP6S11}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b Not once but twice denied by Yeshua. Or was she?}\par
\pard \ql {\b Her extraordinary answer is astonishing: True Lord, yet even the little  dogs, eat the crumbs that fall from their masters' table. She understood the Parable.  Yeshua wasnt claiming that the little house dogs would not eat. But that the bread was first given to the children. And she did not miss any detail of what he was saying.}\par
\pard \ql {\b She affirms the fact the Yeshua came to the house of Israel. They had the priority. They had the Covenants. She is not offended. She accepts Gods plan.  And she also struck at the very heart of the Gospel: that the kingdom of God overflows to the gentiles; and faith is the means by which that abundance is received.}\par
\pard \ql {\b This Canaanite woman  possessed a kingdom perception that the experts of the Law failed to grasp. The children ate and were satisfiedbut even then, there was an abundance of leftovers.}\par
\pard \ql {\b Moved by this amazing womans response Yeshua declared: O Woman, Great is your faith. May it be to you as you wish. And in an instant, her daughter was healed. The unclean spirit was cast out.}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b \u187?EP6S15}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b There are only two people in Scripture that Yeshua praised for their Great faith: a Roman centurion Soldier pleading for his young servant, and this Canaanite woman pleading for her daughter.}\par
\pard \ql {\b Both were Gentiles. Both understood significant spiritual truth -- in contrast to those who should have.}\par
\pard \ql {\b And it was in them, away from the crowds, that Yeshua found the faith he was looking for.}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b \u187?EP6S21}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b The disciples collected those baskets and carried them in their boat. But in the next scene, Mark tells us something striking: the disciples were terrified when a storm hit, because they did not understand the miracle of the bread. Their hearts were hardened.}\par
\pard \ql {\b \u187?}\par
\pard \ql {\b EP6S22}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b It was right after this exchange, that this Canaanite woman appeared, asking for leftover bread from the childrens table. Do you see the thread? Multiplied bread. Leftover bread.}\par
\pard \ql {\b She realized something important : Yeshuas bread is abundant. There is more than enough for Israel and the nations. And even one crumb of His power is enough to heal a demontormented child. No wonder Yeshua marveled at her Faith.}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b \u187?EP6S24}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b Yeshua does not run out of blessings. There is more than enough bread. Yeshua claimed: I am the bread of life; whoever comes to me shall not hunger.}\par
\pard \ql {\b That promise is not only for the children of Israel. It is for every desperate, hungry soul willing to come to Him in faith.}\par
\pard \ql {\b The Canaanite woman understood something profound: there are many blessings falling off the table for anyone who believes. You may feel like you have no right to ask. But this woman teaches us that if you believe in the bread of life himself, you will be satisfied.}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b \u187?}\par
\pard \ql {\b EP6S30}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b Did this unnamed woman open a door? Her faith -- the faith of a Gentile woman became the bridge for a large crowd celebrating Bread and being satisfied.}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b \u187?EP7: WOMAN WITH SPIRIT OF INFIRMITY}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b EP7S5}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b Then he  stopped and declared: Today this has been fulfilled. The acceptable year of the Lord is now happening among you. It is being fulfilled now.}\par
\pard \ql {\b In the Torah the acceptable year of the Lord is the year of Jubilee. It is also called the Sabbath of Sabaths -- when all debts would be canceled all slaves freed, and each one would return to his original inheritance.  (Lev.25)}\par
\pard \ql {\b By declaring that the acceptable year of the Lord was now being fulfilled, Yeshua was revealing that He is the anointed one spoken of in this passage. These were not just beautiful words to read, but a living reality happening wherever Yeshua encountered people.}\par
\pard \ql {\b \u187?}\par
\pard \ql {\b EP7S12}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b By loosing the woman with the spirit that bound her and kept her bent over, Yeshua was revealing Himself as the living Jubilee. The one who brings ultimate freedom and rest.}\par
\pard \ql {\b Jubilee is not simply an ancient command; it is the heartbeat of the gospel. Its the ongoing work of Gods kingdom in the world today. Yeshua came to cancel our debts, set us free from bondage, and restore us to himself.}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b \u187?EP7S18}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b As soon as Yeshua declared that she was free, the woman stood straight up, and glorified God.}\par
\pard \ql {\b The scene powerfully echoes Davids words of rejoicing from Psalm 145: The Lord upholds all who are falling and raises up all who are bowed down.}\par
\pard \ql {\b Yes, when people are set free and raised up, the response is praise. Its a true and natural outburst of the hearts gratitude.}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b \u187?}\par
\pard \ql {\b EP7S25}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b The word Yeshua used for untying an animal is the same word for loosed. Yeshua is making his point clear: The religious leaders made rules that allowed them to untie their animals from its rope on the Sabbath without a second thought. Yet this woman, a daughter of Abraham, bound for 18 years with a severe affliction should wait? Was she not worth more than an animal??}\par
\pard \ql {\b This is the only place in the Gospels where Yeshua refers to a woman as a daughter of Abraham. In the Torah, Abrahams children were precious. They had promises and blessings bestowed on them. By calling her daughter of Abraham, He was showing the value and the dignity he has for her. She was a woman of the covenant: Worthy of every blessing.}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b \u187?EP7S31}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b Just as the woman in the synagogue was bent and bound for many years, we too can find ourselves carrying burdens that weigh us down. The enemy works subtly  not always by dramatic attacks, but by binding our minds and hearts with lies about our worth, our identity, and our place before God. We may feel spiritually bent over, unable to lift our heads, believing we are less than who God says we are.}\par
\pard \ql {\b But Yeshua sees us even when we cannot see Him clearly. He calls us, not to shame us, but to free us.}{\b   In Him, you are not overlooked, diminished, or defined by your struggles. You are deeply valued. And where the enemy seeks to bind, Yeshua comes to release.}\par
\pard \ql {\b Come to me.  This is a call to you today  you who are bent over, heavy burdened, oppressed, or bound. Maybe you cant even lift your eyes toward Him, but when He calls you, you can draw near with confidence.}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b \u187?EP7S34}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b Throughout this series, weve met women that the world left nameless. And in every story, Yeshua did the same thing. He saw them and he gave them back their identity. Thats who he is, and thats what he does. And thats what he is doing still.}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b \u187?EP8: ADULTEROUS WOMAN}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b EP8S4}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b Isaiah had spoken of a time of great darkness, when people would be walking in the shadow of Death. But he also brought words of hope. A great light would arrive in the land of Israel and his appearance would bring increase of joy.}\par
\pard \ql {\b Why is Isaiah so focused on Joy that he mentions it four times in one verse?}{\b  }{\b Because!!!!! And in the next verse he tells us the reason. T}{\b his great light will break the power of the enemy that enslaves the people. He will free them from the oppression of the enemy who rules over them. Only the great light will be able to defeat even the strongest enemy and free us from its power. It is no wonder that there is great joy among all the people.}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b \u187?}\par
\pard \ql {\b Rewrite}{\b : Isaiah so focused on Joy that he goes on to mention it four times in one verse. Why? Because this great light would break the power of the enemy that enslaves the people. He will free them from the oppression of the enemy who rules over them. Only the great light will be able to defeat even the strongest enemy and free us from its power. It is no wonder that there is great joy among all the people.}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b \u187?}\par
\pard \ql {\b EP8S5}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b But who is this great light?  V.6 assures the readers that this great light is a person.}\par
\pard \ql {\b For unto us a child is born unto us a son is given and the government shall be on his shoulders, and his name will be called wonderful counsellor mighty god everlasting father prince of peace (v.6). The great light is the child born to us: he was the son given to us. Congratulations!!! You and I have been given the greatest gift the world has ever known.}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b \u187?}\par
\pard \ql {\b EP8S14}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b Before the Messiah was born, the angel Gabriel told Joseph to name his son Yeshua, and he explained the reason: because he would save his people from their sins. Saving people from their sins is more than forgiving people their sins. It means to save them from the power that causes them to sin. This is very good news.}\par
\pard \ql {\b \u187?}\par
\pard \ql {\b EP8S15}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b You see sin is stronger than we are. Later on in this chapter Yeshua said: Everyone who commits sin is a slave to sin. The slave does not remain in the house forever; the son remains forever. So if the Son sets you free, you will be free indeed. "}\par
\pard \ql {\b There is something within us that causes us to sin again and again. It is sin ruling over us. To save us from our sins means to free us from this force that causes us to sin over and over.}\par
\pard \ql {\b \u187?}\par
\pard \ql {\b EP8S23}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b Are you struggling with guilt? Is your past causing you shame? Do you feel unable to overcome sin in your life? Some of us might be bleeding from wounds that keep you isolated. Or maybe like the Canaanite woman you have a very desperate situation that causes you to call out for help.}\par
\pard \ql {\b These women are here for a reason.  They represent all of us. Their stories teach us powerful truth about the character of God our father. He sees you. He loves you and deeply cares for you. He crossed all boundaries to give you life and to satisfy your soul with an everlasting relationship.}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b \u187?EP8S24}\par
\par
\pard \ql {\b TO BE WRITTEN}\par
\par
